# Shell scripts must keep LF. Without this, a checkout on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites a .sh to CRLF, and bash then fails on line 2 with
# "$'\r': command not found" -- a repository defect that only appears on a machine
# that checked the file out rather than cloned it on Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The SQL test suites and migrations are fed to psql via stdin. CRLF is tolerated
# there, but pinning them removes a class of "works locally, fails in CI".
*.sql text eol=lf

# Corpus snapshots are named by the SHA-256 of their own contents. If Git
# rewrites the line endings on checkout, the file no longer hashes to the name it
# carries and verify-corpus.mjs reports "the snapshot was modified in place" --
# which is true, and not what happened. On Windows core.autocrlf=true is the
# default, so this fired on every checkout. A content-addressed scheme and an
# automatic newline translation are incompatible; one of them has to be pinned.
tooling/corpus/snapshots/*.json text eol=lf
tooling/corpus/manifest.json text eol=lf
tooling/corpus/translation-pairs.json text eol=lf

# The skill's frontmatter is parsed by tooling/verify-agent-skill.mjs, which was
# CRLF-sensitive and failed on every Windows checkout until it was fixed. It is
# fixed, and the file is still pinned to LF so the parser is not the only thing
# standing between a checkout and a red gate.
Hatem_specific.md text eol=lf
